1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,780
What happened yesterday? You woke up.
You had breakfast. You talked to someone.

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,130
Maybe you went to work.

3
00:00:06,898 --> 00:00:09,318
And now? None of those
things are happening anymore.

4
00:00:09,398 --> 00:00:12,488
But they happened. You know they happened.

5
00:00:13,291 --> 00:00:15,281
So here is a strange question.

6
00:00:16,051 --> 00:00:17,461
Where is yesterday now?

7
00:00:17,891 --> 00:00:19,071
Is it somewhere in the universe?

8
00:00:19,151 --> 00:00:20,721
Did it simply disappear?

9
00:00:21,647 --> 00:00:24,107
Or does the past still
exist, in some sense,

10
00:00:24,187 --> 00:00:26,737
even though we can no
longer experience it?

11
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:32,997
Before we can answer that, we need
a much smaller question first.

12
00:00:33,867 --> 00:00:35,817
What does it mean for something to exist?

13
00:00:36,861 --> 00:00:40,211
Suppose you have a photograph of
yourself from twenty years ago.

14
00:00:41,041 --> 00:00:42,391
The photograph exists.

15
00:00:43,161 --> 00:00:45,431
It is sitting on your computer right now.

16
00:00:46,156 --> 00:00:48,066
But the person in that photograph?

17
00:00:48,836 --> 00:00:52,106
That particular moment of your
life is not happening anymore.

18
00:00:52,889 --> 00:00:54,679
The photograph is not the past.

19
00:00:55,569 --> 00:00:59,159
It is an object in the present,
carrying information about the past.

20
00:01:00,009 --> 00:01:02,839
And once you notice that, you
start seeing it everywhere.

21
00:01:03,762 --> 00:01:05,472
A fossil is not the dinosaur.

22
00:01:06,462 --> 00:01:08,212
Ash is not the tree that burned.

23
00:01:08,822 --> 00:01:11,112
A memory in your head is not yesterday.

24
00:01:11,905 --> 00:01:16,475
They are all traces. Marks left
behind by something that happened.

25
00:01:17,293 --> 00:01:18,683
And traces are thin.

26
00:01:19,553 --> 00:01:21,343
Ash tells you that there was a tree.

27
00:01:22,173 --> 00:01:24,353
It does not tell you
which tree, or how tall it

28
00:01:24,433 --> 00:01:27,143
stood, or what the light
looked like underneath it.

29
00:01:27,761 --> 00:01:30,251
Most of what happens
leaves nothing at all.

30
00:01:31,036 --> 00:01:34,046
What colour was the sky at
three o'clock last Tuesday?

31
00:01:34,796 --> 00:01:37,446
How many steps did you
take the day before that?

32
00:01:38,217 --> 00:01:40,047
Both of those were real events.

33
00:01:40,777 --> 00:01:42,387
They had a definite answer.

34
00:01:43,177 --> 00:01:45,447
And now there may be no
trace of it anywhere.

35
00:01:46,274 --> 00:01:48,744
Which gives us an
uncomfortable possibility.

36
00:01:49,574 --> 00:01:51,244
Maybe the past does not exist.

37
00:01:52,114 --> 00:01:55,704
Maybe all that exists is the small
amount it happened to leave behind.

38
00:01:58,844 --> 00:02:03,134
Look at the Sun. What you see is
not the Sun as it is right now.

39
00:02:03,604 --> 00:02:06,534
You see the Sun as it was
about eight minutes ago.

40
00:02:07,307 --> 00:02:10,317
That is simply how long its
light takes to reach us.

41
00:02:10,987 --> 00:02:13,177
And it gets much more extreme than that.

42
00:02:13,911 --> 00:02:16,741
When you look at a galaxy
a billion light years away,

43
00:02:16,911 --> 00:02:19,621
you are seeing it as it
was a billion years ago.

44
00:02:20,322 --> 00:02:21,712
Not a picture of it.

45
00:02:21,802 --> 00:02:23,712
Not a recording of it.

46
00:02:24,442 --> 00:02:28,632
That is the actual light, arriving today,
after a journey of a billion years.

47
00:02:29,553 --> 00:02:32,623
So astronomy is not really
the study of distant things.

48
00:02:33,613 --> 00:02:35,243
It is the study of old things.

49
00:02:36,093 --> 00:02:39,243
The farther away we look, the
deeper into the past we see.

50
00:02:39,978 --> 00:02:42,988
The screen in front of you is
a few nanoseconds in the past.

51
00:02:43,918 --> 00:02:47,128
The far wall of the room, a
little further back than that.

52
00:02:47,884 --> 00:02:50,354
You have never once seen
anything in the present.

53
00:02:51,124 --> 00:02:54,954
Everything you have ever seen is a record
of something that already happened.

54
00:02:57,714 --> 00:03:00,884
So the universe is full of
information about its own past.

55
00:03:01,735 --> 00:03:04,385
But look carefully at
what that information is.

56
00:03:04,995 --> 00:03:08,685
It is light arriving today.
A signal in the present.

57
00:03:09,601 --> 00:03:12,731
And information about the past
is still not the past itself.

58
00:03:15,841 --> 00:03:17,811
Now there is a much bigger problem.

59
00:03:18,721 --> 00:03:20,641
If light takes time to travel, what does

60
00:03:20,721 --> 00:03:23,051
right now even mean
for the whole universe?

61
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:27,810
What is happening right now on a
planet a thousand light years away?

62
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:31,030
Relativity gives us no single answer.

63
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:33,718
Two observers moving
differently can disagree

64
00:03:33,798 --> 00:03:36,908
about which distant events
happen at the same time.

65
00:03:37,758 --> 00:03:39,268
Neither of them is wrong.

66
00:03:39,678 --> 00:03:42,088
Neither of them is measuring badly.

67
00:03:42,995 --> 00:03:46,965
There is no single universal now
shared by the entire universe.

68
00:03:47,827 --> 00:03:49,557
And that is awkward for us.

69
00:03:50,227 --> 00:03:54,997
Because if there is no universal present,
what exactly separates the past from it?

70
00:03:57,337 --> 00:04:00,047
One possible answer is
called the block universe.

71
00:04:01,017 --> 00:04:06,677
Imagine a film. Watching it, you get the
story one frame at a time. Beginning.

72
00:04:06,757 --> 00:04:07,987
Middle. End.

73
00:04:08,799 --> 00:04:11,509
But the film itself is a
complete strip of frames.

74
00:04:12,539 --> 00:04:16,049
The ending does not come into
existence at the moment you reach it.

75
00:04:16,579 --> 00:04:18,329
It was there the whole time.

76
00:04:19,033 --> 00:04:21,383
The block universe is a little like that.

77
00:04:22,193 --> 00:04:25,173
Instead of a universe being
built moment by moment, picture

78
00:04:25,253 --> 00:04:28,683
the whole of its history as
one four-dimensional structure.

79
00:04:29,563 --> 00:04:31,193
Past events are part of it.

80
00:04:31,483 --> 00:04:33,593
Present events are part of it.

81
00:04:33,983 --> 00:04:35,783
Future events are part of it.

82
00:04:35,863 --> 00:04:37,553
Nothing has to move through it.

83
00:04:38,163 --> 00:04:39,633
The structure simply is.

84
00:04:40,459 --> 00:04:43,769
On that picture, your childhood
is not something that vanished.

85
00:04:44,699 --> 00:04:46,549
It is a different part of spacetime.

86
00:04:47,341 --> 00:04:49,231
Which is a strange kind of comfort.

87
00:04:49,461 --> 00:04:52,071
And also a strange kind of threat.

88
00:04:52,541 --> 00:04:56,111
Because it would mean that tomorrow's
version of you is already there.

89
00:04:56,828 --> 00:05:00,078
But be careful here.
This is one possibility.

90
00:05:00,768 --> 00:05:02,438
Relativity makes it attractive.

91
00:05:03,548 --> 00:05:05,358
Relativity does not prove it.

92
00:05:08,038 --> 00:05:11,748
Because there are other answers, and
philosophers have names for them.

93
00:05:12,401 --> 00:05:14,011
The first one is presentism.

94
00:05:14,161 --> 00:05:18,071
Only the present exists. The past is gone.

95
00:05:18,781 --> 00:05:20,531
The future has not happened yet.

96
00:05:21,313 --> 00:05:23,413
It matches how everything feels.

97
00:05:23,493 --> 00:05:26,283
But it has to answer a hard question.

98
00:05:27,233 --> 00:05:28,973
If observers cannot agree on what is

99
00:05:29,053 --> 00:05:31,483
happening now, what
exactly is the present?

100
00:05:32,379 --> 00:05:34,249
The second one is eternalism.

101
00:05:34,859 --> 00:05:37,309
Past, present and future all exist.

102
00:05:38,359 --> 00:05:41,109
Our sense of now is just where
we happen to be standing.

103
00:05:41,929 --> 00:05:43,779
That fits naturally with relativity.

104
00:05:45,029 --> 00:05:47,889
But it has to explain why
we only ever experience one

105
00:05:47,969 --> 00:05:51,259
thin slice of it, and always
in the same direction.

106
00:05:52,161 --> 00:05:54,131
The third one sits between them.

107
00:05:54,681 --> 00:05:56,311
It is called the growing block.

108
00:05:57,208 --> 00:06:00,138
The past exists. The present exists.

109
00:06:00,308 --> 00:06:01,748
The future does not.

110
00:06:01,828 --> 00:06:04,618
Reality is a block that
keeps getting bigger.

111
00:06:05,393 --> 00:06:08,863
Which sounds reasonable, until
you ask where its edge is.

112
00:06:09,733 --> 00:06:13,063
And relativity gives us no
universal way to define it.

113
00:06:13,769 --> 00:06:15,919
Notice what physics has done here.

114
00:06:16,629 --> 00:06:18,219
It did not settle the argument.

115
00:06:18,909 --> 00:06:20,419
It made the argument harder.

116
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:25,140
Relativity makes a single universal
now very difficult to defend.

117
00:06:25,790 --> 00:06:29,300
But relativity does not tell
us what the word exist means.

118
00:06:29,990 --> 00:06:31,660
That part is still philosophy.

119
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:36,850
And it is worth asking honestly.

120
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:40,470
Suppose we say that
yesterday's breakfast exists.

121
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:42,750
What are we actually claiming?

122
00:06:43,527 --> 00:06:47,897
Is it somewhere in space? Can
we go there? Can we measure it?

123
00:06:48,327 --> 00:06:50,097
Can anything about it still change?

124
00:06:51,029 --> 00:06:52,879
Not in any way we can access.

125
00:06:53,809 --> 00:06:57,179
The only thing we can point at
is information available today.

126
00:06:57,997 --> 00:07:00,327
Which takes us straight
back to the photograph.

127
00:07:01,229 --> 00:07:02,819
The photo is not the event.

128
00:07:03,329 --> 00:07:05,019
The memory is not the event.

129
00:07:05,289 --> 00:07:08,479
The light from a galaxy
is not the galaxy's past.

130
00:07:09,389 --> 00:07:12,639
And yet every one of them points at
something that really did happen.

131
00:07:15,479 --> 00:07:20,349
So here is a question that pulls apart
two ideas we normally keep glued together.

132
00:07:21,164 --> 00:07:25,394
Imagine that you wake up tomorrow and
every record of yesterday is gone.

133
00:07:26,167 --> 00:07:28,817
No photographs. No documents.

134
00:07:28,987 --> 00:07:31,277
No memory of it, in any head.

135
00:07:31,407 --> 00:07:34,677
No physical trace anywhere,
down to the last atom.

136
00:07:35,527 --> 00:07:37,577
Would yesterday have stopped existing?

137
00:07:40,637 --> 00:07:42,917
Or would it still have
happened, even though

138
00:07:42,997 --> 00:07:45,547
nothing in the universe
remembered that it did?

139
00:07:46,301 --> 00:07:49,311
And yet we already assume
this, quietly, all the time.

140
00:07:50,045 --> 00:07:51,965
Somebody in a Bronze Age village had a

141
00:07:52,045 --> 00:07:54,935
conversation one evening
and then went to bed.

142
00:07:55,725 --> 00:07:57,755
Nothing recorded a word of it.

143
00:07:58,442 --> 00:08:01,672
We do not conclude that the
conversation never took place.

144
00:08:02,442 --> 00:08:04,492
We conclude that we cannot reach it.

145
00:08:05,175 --> 00:08:07,025
And that is two different things.

146
00:08:07,916 --> 00:08:09,136
Whether the past exists.

147
00:08:09,216 --> 00:08:12,506
And whether there is any
evidence that it does.

148
00:08:13,347 --> 00:08:15,137
Those are not the same question.

149
00:08:16,027 --> 00:08:20,557
And almost everything that feels obvious
about time quietly assumes that they are.

150
00:08:23,587 --> 00:08:25,477
So. Does the past still exist?

151
00:08:26,478 --> 00:08:31,648
Maybe not. Maybe only the present is real,
and the universe keeps making new moments.

152
00:08:32,505 --> 00:08:36,545
Maybe it does. Maybe the whole
history of everything sits there in

153
00:08:36,625 --> 00:08:39,955
spacetime, and today is
simply where we are standing.

154
00:08:40,752 --> 00:08:43,162
Or maybe the difference
between gone and still

155
00:08:43,392 --> 00:08:45,542
there does not work the
way we think it does.

156
00:08:46,331 --> 00:08:47,531
We don't know.

157
00:08:49,904 --> 00:08:53,414
But there is something oddly
reassuring about one of those answers.

158
00:08:54,605 --> 00:08:56,405
Maybe the things that
happened to us do not

159
00:08:56,485 --> 00:08:58,855
have to keep happening
in order to be real.

160
00:08:59,568 --> 00:09:00,768
They happened.

161
00:09:01,178 --> 00:09:02,628
And perhaps that is enough.
